# PROJECT GUIDELINES

## Modular Business Operations, Sales, Inventory, Delivery & Collections Platform

**Document Type:** Project-wide engineering and business requirements\
**Applies To:** All AI agents, developers, QA engineers, and technical contributors\
**Architecture:** Modular Monolith\
**Backend:** Laravel, using the version established by the repository\
**Document Status:** Initial project baseline — pending confirmation of unresolved business rules

---

# 1\. PROJECT MISSION

Build a reliable, secure, maintainable business operations platform that manages the complete commercial lifecycle, from customer visits and sales requests to quotations, purchase orders, sales orders, inventory, deliveries, invoicing, collections, and reporting.

The platform must provide a consistent source of truth for business records and their relationships.

The objective is not to build disconnected CRUD screens. The objective is to implement complete, traceable business workflows with controlled state transitions, accurate calculations, appropriate authorization, and verifiable outcomes.

Every implemented feature must serve a defined business requirement.

All agents must understand the overall business lifecycle before modifying individual modules.

## Primary objectives

- Centralize customer, product, sales, inventory, delivery, and financial records.
- Establish clear responsibilities and permissions for each user role.
- Track the lifecycle and status of every important business document.
- Support partial fulfillment of approved customer purchase orders.
- Maintain accurate quantities, prices, margins, invoice balances, and collections.
- Prevent unauthorized approvals and invalid business state transitions.
- Maintain an auditable history of important actions and document changes.
- Integrate email and WhatsApp Business through reliable, traceable workflows.
- Provide accurate dashboards and reports based on authoritative records.
- Deliver the system incrementally, with a usable and testable result at each stage.

---

# 2\. NON-NEGOTIABLE PROJECT PRINCIPLES

All agents must follow these rules.

## 2.1 Understand before implementing

Before making changes, inspect the repository, current implementation, project rules, relevant requirements, and existing tests.

Do not assume that a feature is missing simply because it is not immediately visible. Search the codebase first.

Do not replace existing working implementations without understanding their purpose and dependencies.

## 2.2 Business rules are authoritative

The approved business requirements define the expected behavior.

Do not invent rules for pricing, margins, credit limits, payment terms, document approval, inventory allocation, or financial closure.

If a required rule has not been approved, document the ambiguity and request clarification when it blocks a safe implementation.

When possible, continue work on independent, well-defined tasks without making consequential assumptions.

## 2.3 Architecture must remain coherent

Use a Modular Monolith: one application with clearly separated business modules and explicit boundaries.

Avoid premature microservices, duplicated business logic, unnecessary abstraction layers, and uncontrolled cross-module dependencies.

## 2.4 Data integrity is mandatory

Important business quantities and financial balances must be derived from authoritative records and validated business operations.

Do not rely on frontend calculations or user-supplied totals as the final source of truth.

Do not permit invalid state transitions, unauthorized changes, or inconsistent document relationships.

## 2.5 Security is part of every feature

Every module must enforce server-side authentication, authorization, validation, and appropriate data visibility.

A hidden button or disabled interface control is not an authorization mechanism.

## 2.6 Every important workflow must be testable

New business logic must have appropriate automated tests.

Test positive behavior, invalid transitions, permissions, boundary conditions, failure handling, and relevant regression scenarios.

## 2.7 Never claim unverified completion

Agents must report what was actually implemented, tested, and verified.

A screen existing does not mean its workflow is complete. A test being present does not mean the behavior is correct. A successful build does not establish release readiness.

