# 6\. CRITICAL BUSINESS INVARIANTS

The following requirements must be enforced by the backend and, where appropriate, the database.

## 6.1 Customer purchase orders and sales orders

A customer purchase order may support multiple sales orders when fulfillment occurs in separate batches.

For each purchase order line:

- Preserve the approved quantity.
- Track the quantity allocated to valid sales orders.
- Track the quantity fulfilled through delivery records.
- Track the remaining quantity accurately.
- Prevent sales orders from exceeding the approved quantity.
- Prevent concurrent operations from allocating the same remaining quantity more than once.
- Preserve the relationship between the original purchase order, sales orders, deliveries, and invoices.

Do not assume that ordered, allocated, prepared, delivered, and invoiced quantities are interchangeable.

Define each quantity precisely.

A canceled or rejected sales order must affect reserved or allocated quantities according to the approved cancellation policy.

A later delivery batch must not require falsifying or replacing the history of earlier batches.

## 6.2 Inventory

Inventory changes must be traceable through authorized inventory operations.

- Record receipts, issues, adjustments, and relevant reservations.
- Prevent unauthorized stock modifications.
- Prevent overselling or overshipping when the applicable inventory policy prohibits it.
- Protect against concurrent allocation and deduction errors.
- Use database transactions and appropriate locking or atomic operations where necessary.
- Preserve inventory movement history.
- Do not silently rewrite historical movements to correct a mistake.

Distinguish physical on-hand quantity, reserved quantity, and available quantity whenever the business process requires them.

Do not finalize the inventory accounting model until reservation, cancellation, delivery, and adjustment rules are approved.

## 6.3 Pricing and margins

- Store commercial prices and approved terms as controlled business records.
- Enforce the approved pricing and margin authorization rules.
- Prevent sales representatives from bypassing required approvals.
- Preserve the approved quotation values and revision history.
- Define how supplier costs, discounts, taxes, rounding, and margin percentages are calculated.
- Do not assume the meaning of "margin" without confirmation.

For example, gross margin and markup are different calculations. The business owner must approve which calculation applies.

## 6.4 Delivery and invoicing

The default business requirement is that an invoice can be issued only after the required signed delivery evidence has been recorded and validated.

- Each delivered batch must be traceable to its sales order.
- Each invoice must reference the relevant delivery and quantities.
- Invoice quantities must not exceed the quantities eligible for invoicing.
- Partial deliveries must be invoiced according to the approved partial-invoicing policy.
- Duplicate invoicing of the same delivered quantity must be prevented.
- Failed invoice creation must not leave inconsistent financial records.
- Corrections must use an approved amendment, credit-note, cancellation, or equivalent accounting workflow where applicable.

The exact legal and accounting requirements must be confirmed for the operating jurisdiction.

## 6.5 Collections and balances

- Record collections as distinct financial transactions.
- Associate collections with the appropriate customer and financial documents.
- Support partial and full collection.
- Track the remaining balance accurately.
- Prevent over-allocation or duplicate posting according to approved policy.
- Preserve the history of payment allocations and reversals.
- Calculate overdue status from approved due dates and outstanding balances.
- Do not mark a transaction financially closed merely because a payment was initiated or a message was sent.

The default closure rule is that the relevant commercial transaction is not financially closed until all required outstanding amounts are settled and all required closure conditions are satisfied.

The business must explicitly define treatment of credit notes, write-offs, disputed amounts, refunds, and other exceptions.

## 6.6 Credit approval

- Credit limits and payment terms require the appropriate authorization.
- Record the approved values and relevant approval history.
- Do not allow an unauthorized user to change an approved credit profile.
- Define whether credit exposure includes outstanding invoices, open orders, or other commitments.
- Define how credit availability is checked when multiple orders are created concurrently.

Never invent the credit exposure formula.

